== Output constraints ==

What are the maximum theoretical resolutions possible on the N64?

The VI defines signal bounds through <code>VI_H_TOTAL</code>, <code>VI_V_TOTAL</code>, and associated sync and burst parameters. The left boundary of active video is set by the end of the colorburst interval (<code>VI_BURST</code>); extending the display window past this point overlaps H_BURST and disrupts chroma reference timing and/or black-level clamping.<ref>The tables in this section assume standard Libultra/Libdragon <code>VI_H_TOTAL</code> values.</ref>

=== Horizontal maxima (VI pixels) ===

{| class="wikitable" style="text-align:center;"
! Standard !! VI_H_TOTAL !! Effective htotal !! Burst start !! Burst width !! Burst end !! Max Addressable Width<ref>Calculated as htotal - (burst_start + burst_width). This is the theoretical maximum safe width.</ref>
|-
| '''NTSC''' || <code>0x0C15</code> || 773 || 62 || 34 || 96 || '''677'''
|-
| '''PAL'''  || <code>0x0C69</code> || 794 || 64 || 35 || 99 || '''695'''
|-
| '''MPAL''' || <code>0x0C10</code> || 772 || 70 || 30 || 100 || '''672'''
|}

Calculations:
* NTSC: htotal = (<code>0x0C15</code> + 1) / 4 = 3094 / 4 = 773.5 → 773
* PAL:  (<code>0x0C69</code> + 1) / 4 = 3178 / 4 = 794.5 → 794
* MPAL: (<code>0x0C10</code> + 1) / 4 = 3089 / 4 = 772.25 → 772

